<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for SentryWatch (Sentry for Apple Watch). The watch app is
  embedded in the iPhone app's upload, so the same App Store Connect gate
  applies to it.

  This target's surface is genuinely small, and the manifest is short because
  the audit found little, not because it was skipped. What compiles in: the
  Swift files under SentryWatch/, plus SentryKit.framework built from
  `SentryKit_watchOS` — which, unlike its macOS/iOS siblings, does not take
  the whole SentryKit directory. project.yml enumerates it file by file:
  SentryKit/Watch/ (the whole directory — RelayedPalette, WatchRelayPolicy,
  WatchRelaySnapshot, WatchRelayStore), plus Sync/Freshness.swift,
  Sync/FreshnessBadge.swift, Sync/SyncModels.swift, Settings/Theme.swift,
  Settings/ThemeTokens.swift, Settings/ThemeContrast.swift and
  Models/MetricID.swift — eleven files from seven path entries.

  (That count is stated precisely because it is the whole argument for what
  is absent below, and it has drifted before: the list grew when the watch
  gained themed rendering and Siri intents, while the prose here and in
  project.yml still said "four files." Re-count it against project.yml
  before trusting it again.)

  So the two required-reason call sites the iPhone side has to declare —
  `ProcessInfo.processInfo.systemUptime` in Insights/DeviceFacts.swift, and
  the `UserDefaults.standard` default in Persistence/RollupJob.swift —
  are not in this binary at all, and are
  correctly not declared here. Of the eleven files above, only
  Watch/WatchRelayStore.swift touches a required-reason API, and it is the
  App Group suite declared below. Models/MetricID.swift is the one that looks
  like a counterexample and is not: its `case systemUptimeSeconds` is a
  String-backed enum identifier, never a call to read the boot time.

  Reason-code text quoted below comes from Apple's
  "NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons" documentation:
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/app-privacy-configuration/nsprivacyaccessedapitypes/nsprivacyaccessedapitypereasons
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  There is no networking code on the watch. `WatchRelayManager`'s doc
	  comment records why: no Bonjour/Network.framework path to the Mac exists
	  from watchOS in this app, so everything the watch shows arrives from the
	  paired iPhone over `WCSession` — a device-to-device Apple transport, not
	  a host this app dials.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  Nothing. `WatchRelaySnapshot` (SentryKit/Watch/WatchRelaySnapshot.swift)
	  is a device name, two timestamps, a demo-data flag, battery
	  percent/charging/plugged-in, and a thermal-pressure summary. It carries
	  no location (the app collects none at all) and no
	  identifier of any kind.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<!--
			  1C8F.1 — "Declare this reason to access user defaults to read
			  and write information only accessible to the apps, app
			  extensions, and App Clips that are members of the same App
			  Group as the app itself."
			  Evidence: `WatchRelayStore`
			  (SentryKit/Watch/WatchRelayStore.swift) opens
			  `UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.dev.malekswilam.sentry")` — the
			  group in SentryWatch/SentryWatch.entitlements. It is written
			  by `WatchSessionController.session(_:didReceive...)` on every
			  relay from the phone and read back in that controller's init, so
			  the complication process can find the payload even though it may
			  not have been alive when `WCSession` delivered it. That
			  identifier names a container on the Watch itself, distinct from
			  the same-named group on the iPhone.

			  CA92.1 is deliberately not declared: nothing in this bundle
			  touches `UserDefaults.standard` or `@AppStorage`. The App Group
			  suite above is the only defaults access that exists here.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Searched for and genuinely absent: no `ProcessInfo.systemUptime`
		  (DeviceFacts.swift is not in this target's compile set), no disk
		  space API, no file timestamp read, no active-keyboard API.
		-->
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
